[{:db/id 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" :question/id #uuid "320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-71231" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer-field/choices ["answer-652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" "answer-e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" "answer-43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" "answer-047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" "answer-60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" :answer/id #uuid "652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" :answer/id #uuid "e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" :answer/id #uuid "047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712" :answer/id #uuid "60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fa97339f-4dd8-5f3c-87ec-f76f87